Async proxying and MPM queuing: benchmark harness and checks (prototype)

Build the Go tools (Go >= 1.21, no module needed):
  go build -o backend backend.go     # backend: GET /?d=MS answers 512 bytes after MS ms,
                                     # "Upgrade: echo" gets a 101 then an echo,
                                     # GET /?s=BYTES streams BYTES, POST reads its body
  go build -o loadgen loadgen.go     # load generator (closed loop, or open loop -rate;
                                     # paced uploads -upload, slow reads -rchunk)
  go build -o tunnelgen tunnelgen.go # tunnels (Upgrade: echo) sending a message every -i
  go build -o fcgibackend fcgibackend.go # FastCGI backend: GET /?d=MS answers 512 bytes
                                     # after MS ms, /?s=BYTES streams BYTES, POST reads
                                     # its body; -raw ADDR misbehaves (closes early)

Build mod_aptest (its "aptest-queue" handler, controlled service times):
  cd ../pyhttpd/mod_aptest && $PREFIX/bin/apxs -c mod_aptest.c

The scripts start their own httpd from an install prefix ($PREFIX, e.g. built
from this tree) with their own configuration under WORKDIR, on 127.0.0.1
ports 18431-18435 (checks) and 18500-18510 (bench, queue). They pin httpd on
CPUs 0-3 and the tools on 4-7 (taskset); for stable numbers use a fixed CPU
frequency (cpupower frequency-set -g performance, no turbo). CPUS="HTTPD
BACKEND TOOLS" changes the CPUs of the benches (e.g. CPUS="0-1 2 3" on 4
CPUs). The benches using bench.py's helpers and the checks also run on
FreeBSD (cpuset, ps, truss and netstat for taskset, /proc, strace and ss);
queue.py is Linux only.

bench.py PREFIX WORKDIR [delays] [backend_ms]
  ProxyAsyncDelay benchmark: blocking (-1) vs async (0, 1ms, 10ms) against
  backends answering in 0/1/10/100ms, 64 clients on one child of 8 threads;
  throughput, latency percentiles, CPU and context switches per request of
  the httpd tree. POOLMAX sets the backend pool's max= (256 by default, below
  CLIENTS for a pool-bound run where requests wait for a connection); STRACE=1
  adds the httpd child's syscalls per request (strace -c over the measured
  window, which slows it down: only the per-request ratios are meaningful).
  Build httpd optimized for numbers that mean something (-O2, no AP_DEBUG).
  Run it several times into log files, then medians.py LOG... for the median
  of each cell.

tunnelbench.py PREFIX WORKDIR [tunnels,...]
  Tunnel benchmark: 100, 1000 and 5000 tunnels by default (Upgrade: echo
  through mod_proxy_http, async), each sending a SIZE-byte message (64) every
  INTERVAL (1s) to the backend's echo; messages/s, latency percentiles, CPU
  and context switches per message, and syscalls with STRACE=1 (the
  epoll_ctl churn per message). DELAY=-1 for blocking tunnels (one thread
  each, so no more tunnels than ThreadsPerChild). medians.py reads its logs
  too.

bodybench.py PREFIX WORKDIR [delays] [scenarios]
  Large-body benchmark: blocking (-1) vs async (0) by default, 16 clients,
  responses of SIZE bytes (4MB) streamed by the backend in CHUNK-byte writes
  (64KB) and read by clients slower than the backend (down-slow: a CHUNK read
  every PAUSE, 1ms) or as fast as possible (down-fast), and uploads of SIZE
  bytes written a CHUNK every PAUSE (up). On loopback these hardly make a
  request wait more than once (large socket buffers), so down-crawl and
  up-crawl read or send CRAWL_SIZE bytes (1MB) a CRAWL_CHUNK (16KB) every
  CRAWL_PAUSE (20ms), down-crawl through the client's receive buffer and
  httpd's SendBufferSize set to CRAWL_CHUNK: about 60 waits per request.
  Requests/s, MB/s, latency percentiles, CPU and context switches per MB of
  the httpd tree, and syscalls per request with STRACE=1. medians.py reads its
  logs too.

queue.py PREFIX MOD_APTEST_SO WORKDIR [scenario...]
  MPM queuing scenarios (burst, overload-cpu, blocked, async-mix, progress,
  two-children): a background load and open-loop probes (a new connection
  each, latency from the scheduled time) against "aptest-queue", with the
  server's backlog stats (mpm_event's "backlog sojourn" lines, LogLevel
  mpm_event:trace2) aggregated over the measured window and httpd's CPU use.
  The admission's target and interval (ms, 5 and 100 by default) are set at
  build time (-DAP_EVENT_ADMISSION_TARGET=, -DAP_EVENT_ADMISSION_INTERVAL=).

checks/async-*-check.py PREFIX WORKDIR
  Functional checks of the async proxy paths, each comparing blocking ("sync")
  and async (ProxyAsyncDelay 0) modes (MODES="sync async" by default):
  proto (response wait), body (response body), send (request body), head
  (response head), connect, pool (waiting for a connection of an exhausted
  pool, max= and acquire=), prefetch (request body before acquiring), tls
  (backend handshake), remote (the CONNECT exchange with a forward proxy,
  ProxyRemote, in front of the tls backend), balancer (failover,
  failonstatus/failontimeout), shutdown (graceful and hard restarts/stops
  with suspended requests), 100 (Expect: 100-continue end-to-end, ping=, ping
  timeout, final responses instead of 100, the 100 sent by httpd itself, and
  uploads waiting for the 100 not holding a worker), tunnel (Upgrade: tunnels
  exchanging data, many on few threads, the idle timeout, either side
  closing, a large transfer to a slow client, Upgrades answered late or
  refused; tunnels being async by default, "sync" is ProxyAsyncDelay -1
  there, and the "default", "wstunnel" (mod_proxy_wstunnel) and "connect"
  (mod_proxy_connect) modes are added), fcgi (mod_proxy_fcgi against
  fcgibackend: waits on few threads, timeout=, large streamed responses,
  request bodies, the backend closing early or down, pipelined requests,
  backend keep-alive with disablereuse=off, flushes (flushpackets=on) and an
  error's ErrorDocument after an async wait to clients that stop reading),
  redirect (an ErrorDocument proxied over http and fcgi: internally
  redirected requests don't suspend; and after an async wait, proxied to the
  same backend reusing its connection).
  PASSOPTS adds ProxyPass options (e.g. max=32 to not be bounded by the
  backend connection pool).
